# CactusFS - the settings of deploy/deploy.sh.
#
# Copy this file to deploy/.env (git ignores it, and it never enters the
# image's build context), or anywhere and pass it with --env FILE. Take the
# "#" off the lines you set. Every key the script reads is here, with its
# default and what it does; a key that is not here is refused.
#
# The file is data, never code. A line is KEY=value; the value is everything
# after the first "=", exactly as written - no quotes are taken off, and no
# "$", backtick or ";" means anything. Write it without quotes, on one line,
# with no comment after it; a comment is a line of its own whose first
# character other than spaces is "#". The one expansion: a leading "~/" in a key that
# ends in _DIR or _FILE is your home directory. A relative path is relative
# to the repository (the directory deploy/ is in). An empty value is the
# default. A flag wins over this file, and this file over the defaults; the
# environment of your shell is not read.
#
# THIS FILE NEVER HOLDS A SECRET. A secret is given as the path of a file
# that holds it, by a key ending in _FILE; leave those empty and the script
# generates the secret once and keeps it in STATE_DIR. A key that looks like
# a secret (ROOT_SECRET_KEY, KMS_PASSPHRASE, *PASSWORD*, *TOKEN* ...) is
# refused.
#
# Settings of the nodes themselves ("cactusfs server", listed in
# deploy/cactusfs.env.example) may be added as CACTUSFS_*=value lines and are
# passed to every node, such as
#
#   CACTUSFS_LOG_LEVEL=debug
#
# - except a secret (by the word of deploy/cactusfs.env.example, or by a
# name with SECRET, PASSWORD, PASSPHRASE, TOKEN or CREDENTIAL in it), a
# setting the script sets itself (addresses, drives, peers, certificates,
# the KMS ...: the list is in deploy/lib/env.sh), and a value that holds a
# "$", a backtick or a control character, which no setting of the nodes
# takes.
#
# Names and addresses in the examples below are placeholders (example.test,
# 192.0.2.0/24); none of them is a real host.

# ---- What and where ----

# The platform: dd (Docker Desktop), ce (Docker CE, on this machine or on
# CE_HOST), podman, rancher (Rancher Desktop), k3s or k8s. --docker wins.
# Empty: the target of what already runs under DEPLOY_NAME (labelled
# containers, or a labelled namespace); refused when nothing or more than
# one target does.
#DEPLOY_TARGET=

# The name of the deployment: lower-case letters, digits and hyphens, at most
# 40. It names and labels everything the script makes, and limits what it
# touches: two deployments with two names live side by side.
#DEPLOY_NAME=cactusfs

# standalone: one node with DRIVES drives (2 data + 2 parity with 4).
# cluster: 3 voting nodes and one more (on Docker, deploy/compose/compose.yaml).
#DEPLOY_MODE=standalone

# ---- Encryption keys (KMS) ----

# Which KMS wraps the data keys: "local", a sealed key file the script makes
# and keeps on the deployment's volume, opened by a passphrase; or "vault",
# HashiCorp Vault Transit (VAULT_* below), which a cluster in production
# uses. It has no default and must be stated: this line is the explicit
# opt-in to the local key file. Back up the passphrase (STATE_DIR): without
# it no object can be read.
KMS=local

# A cluster on one local key file shares it among the nodes. Refused unless
# this says yes; use KMS=vault for a cluster in production.
#ALLOW_SHARED_LOCAL_KMS=no

# Your own sealed key file (a kms.json) and a file holding its passphrase,
# with KMS=local. Empty: the passphrase is generated once into STATE_DIR, and
# the key file is made with it by "cactusfs dev-init" on this machine, into
# STATE_DIR, before anything starts on the target - never by a node. A key
# file needs its passphrase file; a passphrase file alone is used to make
# the key file.
#KMS_KEY_FILE=
#KMS_PASSPHRASE_FILE=

# Vault Transit, with KMS=vault: the address (https only), the mount of the
# Transit engine, the key (an AEAD type such as aes256-gcm96), a file holding
# the token, the CA that issued Vault's certificate (empty: the system's),
# and the Enterprise namespace. On Kubernetes VAULT_ADDR names Vault by its
# IPv4 address (https://192.0.2.20:8200): the NetworkPolicy lets the pods
# reach that address and port, and a NetworkPolicy knows no names.
#VAULT_ADDR=
#VAULT_TRANSIT_MOUNT=transit
#VAULT_KEY=
#VAULT_TOKEN_FILE=
#VAULT_CA_FILE=
#VAULT_NAMESPACE=

# ---- Root credentials ----

# Files holding the access key ID and the secret key of the root credentials
# of a new deployment, both or neither. Neither: a pair is generated once
# into STATE_DIR. Either way they are never on a command line or printed.
#ROOT_ACCESS_KEY_FILE=
#ROOT_SECRET_KEY_FILE=

# ---- State on this machine ----

# Where the script keeps what it generated - secrets, certificates - and
# which engine or cluster each deployment was made on: one directory a
# deployment, <STATE_DIR>/<target-id>/<DEPLOY_NAME> (0700, files 0600), where
# the target-id names the engine, the cluster or the ssh host. A later run
# whose settings point at another engine or cluster is refused; a second
# deployment of one name on another target is said with --another-target.
# Empty: $XDG_STATE_HOME/cactusfs-deploy, or ~/.local/state/cactusfs-deploy.
# A directory inside the repository is refused; one in a folder a sync
# service copies (Documents, Desktop, iCloud, OneDrive, Dropbox) is warned of.
#STATE_DIR=

# yes: never pull or fetch anything public, for an air-gapped site. Every
# image a run needs must then be here already (HELM_IMAGE or HELM, the
# runtime image, IMAGE), and builds do not pull. Refused then: installing
# Docker CE (CE_INSTALL_DOCKER) and building from source (its base images,
# Go modules and npm packages). K8S_REGISTRY, your own registry, stays
# allowed.
#OFFLINE=no

# ---- The image ----

# Where the image comes from:
#   binaries  built from the binaries of "make image-binaries" in RELEASE_DIR
#             (checked against SHA256SUMS and BUILDINFO)
#   source    built from this tree with the Dockerfile (--from-source)
#   image     IMAGE, as it is, deployed by its digest
#IMAGE_SOURCE=binaries
#RELEASE_DIR=dist/image
#IMAGE=

# Build the image on another machine through ssh (user@host), such as one of
# the target's architecture. Empty: on this machine, never emulated.
#BUILD_HOST=

# A tree with uncommitted changes is refused for a target on another machine
# unless this says yes.
#ALLOW_DIRTY=no

# ---- Docker, Podman and Rancher Desktop ----

# The script never follows DOCKER_HOST, DOCKER_CONTEXT, CONTAINER_HOST,
# KUBECONFIG, DOCKER_CONFIG or anything else of your shell's environment:
# every tool runs under "env -i", with PATH, HOME and only what it needs
# (SSH_AUTH_SOCK for ssh, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR for podman; TOOL_ENV_PASS adds
# more). The Docker CLI is
# given this context explicitly. Empty: "default", the local
# daemon (rancher-desktop for Rancher Desktop). For Rancher Desktop's moby a
# context named here must reach Rancher Desktop: "docker info" must give the
# name (.Name) lima-0 - the host of its Lima instance "0" on macOS and Linux
# - or an operating system (.OperatingSystem) naming Rancher Desktop (its WSL
# distribution on Windows); another engine is refused, and the run says what
# the context reached.
#ENGINE_CONTEXT=

# How many drives (labelled named volumes) the standalone node has, 1 to 4.
# With fewer than 4 there is no parity. In cluster mode it stays 4: each of
# compose.yaml's four nodes has two.
#DRIVES=4

# The address S3 is published on, and the admin API and console. The admin
# port stays on the loopback unless you change it: the first sign-in is the
# default console user of ADR-0014. 0.0.0.0 publishes on every address.
# In cluster mode BIND and ADMIN_BIND cannot be set: the ports are those of
# deploy/compose/compose.yaml, S3 on every address, admin on 127.0.0.1.
#BIND=127.0.0.1
#ADMIN_BIND=127.0.0.1

# The published ports, 1024 to 65535 (rootless engines have no lower ones).
# In cluster mode S3_PORT ends in 00 and is node 1's: node 2 is on S3_PORT +
# 10, node 3 on + 20, node 4 on + 30, each admin port the next one, so
# ADMIN_PORT is S3_PORT + 1 (compose.yaml's CACTUSFS_PORT_BASE_1 to _4).
#S3_PORT=9000
#ADMIN_PORT=9001

# ---- Certificates ----

# Names and addresses clients use to reach S3, separated by commas, for the
# development certificate, such as s3.example.test,192.0.2.10. localhost and
# the loopback addresses are always in it.
#TLS_HOSTS=

# How long the development certificates are valid, in hours (24h to
# 87600h). They cannot be renewed by the script: --status shows their
# expiry, and a deployment whose certificates expired needs your own PKI.
#TLS_VALIDITY=8760h

# Your own certificate and key for S3 and the admin API, both or neither,
# and the CA that issued it (for --status). Neither: a development
# certificate is made with "cactusfs dev-init".
#TLS_CERT_FILE=
#TLS_KEY_FILE=
#TLS_CA_FILE=

# Your own CA for the node certificates of a cluster, and a directory with a
# certificate and key a node (<node>.crt, <node>.key). Empty: made by
# "cactusfs dev-init".
#NODE_CA_FILE=
#NODE_CERT_DIR=

# ---- Docker CE on a server ----

# Run on Docker CE on another machine over ssh, such as
# deploy@server.example.test. Empty: this machine. Every ssh the script runs
# (CE_HOST, K3S_SSH, BUILD_HOST) never prompts and never accepts a host key
# it does not know: put the host's key in known_hosts first; the script
# checks it with ssh before it runs anything there. The destination
# is letters, digits and _./=:@+,- only, and never begins with "-"; it names
# no port (give the server a Host entry with HostName and Port in
# ~/.ssh/config, and name that). The lines of known_hosts that accept
# CE_HOST at the first install are pinned in STATE_DIR, and every later ssh
# to it - the Docker CLI's too - accepts it with them alone. The
# admin port stays on the server's 127.0.0.1; --status prints the ssh -L
# command that brings it here. The server needs curl (the script asks with
# it there whether a port is free and the node ready).
#CE_HOST=

# Install Docker CE on CE_HOST first, from Docker's repository - the only
# time the script fetches anything from the internet - when the server has
# no Docker: Debian, Ubuntu, RHEL, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux or CentOS Stream,
# as root or as a user with passwordless sudo. Docker's signing key is
# checked against the fingerprint Docker publishes. Another distribution is
# refused; a host where k3s, k0s, MicroK8s or Kubernetes is, or that has
# podman-docker, is refused unless FORCE says yes.
#CE_INSTALL_DOCKER=no
#CE_INSTALL_DOCKER_FORCE=no

# With CE_INSTALL_DOCKER and a user other than root: add that user to the
# group docker on CE_HOST, which the engine is reached through - and which
# is root on that server. Without it such an install is refused, naming the
# usermod command to run yourself.
#CE_DOCKER_GROUP=no

# The directory of the deployment's files on CE_HOST (cluster mode), relative
# to the home directory there or absolute.
#CE_STATE_DIR=.cactusfs-deploy

# ---- Rancher Desktop ----

# Its engine: auto (moby when the Docker context rancher-desktop - or
# ENGINE_CONTEXT - answers, else containerd when nerdctl does), moby or
# containerd. On moby it is deployed as on Docker Desktop; on containerd
# through nerdctl, standalone only (DEPLOY_MODE=cluster is refused there:
# nerdctl compose cannot upgrade one node at a time).
#RANCHER_ENGINE=auto

# yes: deploy to Rancher Desktop's own Kubernetes (the K8S_* settings apply)
# with the image built locally.
#RANCHER_KUBERNETES=no

# ---- k3s and Kubernetes ----

# A k3s server reached over ssh (user@host): kubectl runs there as
# "k3s kubectl", so its admin kubeconfig never leaves it.
#K3S_SSH=

# THE ONE KEY WHOSE VALUE IS A COMMAND: how an image reaches k3s's
# containerd. The command reads the image as a tar on its standard input,
# such as "docker exec -i k3s-bench k3s ctr -n k8s.io images import -". It
# is split into words at spaces and run as those words, never by a shell:
# letters, digits, spaces and _./=:@+,- only - quotes, $, backticks, ;, |,
# &, <, > and * are refused. Empty: "k3s ctr -n k8s.io images import -" over
# K3S_SSH when it is set, else on this machine (or K8S_REGISTRY when set).
#K3S_IMPORT=

# The kubectl program to run, and the context of your kubeconfig to deploy
# to. --docker k8s needs K8S_CONTEXT: the current context is never assumed.
# k3s without K8S_CONTEXT runs "k3s kubectl", over K3S_SSH or here.
# KUBECTL names a program: no "=" in it.
#KUBECTL=kubectl
#K8S_CONTEXT=

# Every tool the script runs (docker, podman, nerdctl, kubectl, ssh, helm)
# sees only PATH, HOME, LC_ALL=C and what it needs. Names of variables of
# your shell that every tool is given as well, separated by commas - what a
# kubectl exec plugin or a proxy needs, such as
# HTTPS_PROXY,NO_PROXY,AWS_PROFILE,CLOUDSDK_CONFIG. Names only: the values
# come from your shell at each run. Refused: a name that would point a tool
# elsewhere or change how it runs (DOCKER_*, CONTAINER*, KUBECONFIG,
# NERDCTL_*, PODMAN_*, XDG_*, CACTUSFS_*, PATH, HOME, LC_*, SSH_*, LD_*,
# DYLD_*, BASH_ENV, ENV, IFS ...), a name of the script's own, and one that
# looks like a secret (SECRET, PASSWORD, PASSPHRASE, TOKEN, CREDENTIAL).
# Empty: none.
#TOOL_ENV_PASS=

# The namespace, the script's own: made with its labels and the restricted
# Pod Security Standard, removed only by --purge and only when empty.
# Empty: DEPLOY_NAME.
#K8S_NAMESPACE=

# A registry the image is pushed to for the nodes to pull, such as
# registry.example.test/cactusfs. Empty: the image is imported (K3S_IMPORT).
#K8S_REGISTRY=

# pvc: claims of K8S_STORAGE_CLASS (empty: the cluster's default class).
# hostpath: directories on the node, which makes cluster-scoped objects -
# announced, and not for a shared server; only on a cluster of one node.
# K8S_HOSTPATH is the directory on that node, absolute: the volumes are
# <K8S_HOSTPATH>/<namespace>/<node>/<volume>.
#K8S_STORAGE=pvc
#K8S_STORAGE_CLASS=
#K8S_HOSTPATH=/var/lib/cactusfs-deploy

# The size of each drive's claim, and of the metadata's.
#K8S_DRIVE_SIZE=100Gi
#K8S_META_SIZE=10Gi

# How S3 is reached: ClusterIP, NodePort or LoadBalancer. With NodePort or
# LoadBalancer, K8S_S3_ALLOW_FROM is required: CIDRs separated by commas,
# such as 192.0.2.0/24, or "any". An empty K8S_NODE_PORT: the cluster picks.
# Such a Service keeps the client's address (externalTrafficPolicy: Local),
# so the ranges mean clients - and only nodes that run a ready pod answer.
#K8S_SERVICE_TYPE=NodePort
#K8S_NODE_PORT=
#K8S_S3_ALLOW_FROM=

# The admin API and console stay a ClusterIP, closed to the network (use the
# "kubectl port-forward" --status prints), unless both of these say otherwise:
# a NodePort or LoadBalancer type without K8S_ADMIN_ALLOW_FROM is refused.
#K8S_ADMIN_SERVICE_TYPE=ClusterIP
#K8S_ADMIN_NODE_PORT=
#K8S_ADMIN_ALLOW_FROM=

# The image helm renders the chart in, by its digest (...@sha256:<64 hex>):
# a mirrored or preloaded copy. Empty: the one the Dockerfile pins, run with
# docker (ENGINE_CONTEXT) or podman here, with no network and nothing
# mounted. HELM: a helm program on this machine to use instead of a
# container. Not both.
#HELM_IMAGE=
#HELM=

# ---- Waiting and removing ----

# How long, in seconds, a node has to become ready before the script stops.
# (--uninstall --purge asks for the deployment's name at the terminal, or
# takes it from --purge-confirm NAME; that is never a setting of this file.)
#WAIT_TIMEOUT=600
